Mr. Jackson needs to give 2 glue sticks to each of his 27 students. 

First, calculate the total number of glue sticks needed:
$$
27 \text{ students} \times 2 \text{ glue sticks per student} = 54 \text{ glue sticks}
$$

The glue sticks come in packs of 8. To ensure that there are enough glue sticks for all students, we divide the total number of required glue sticks by the number of glue sticks per pack:
$$
\frac{54}{8} = 6.75
$$

Since Mr. Jackson can only buy whole packs and must have enough glue sticks, he needs to round up to the next whole number. Therefore, he must buy:
$$
\lceil 6.75 \rceil = 7
$$

Thus, Mr. Jackson needs to buy $\boxed{7}$ packs of glue sticks. This ensures that there are enough glue sticks for all students, with some left over. 

$$
\boxed{7}
$$

Answer: $\boxed{7}$ packs. 

